{:edb/committed true :edb/basis-t 2091 :edb/replayed false :edb/tx-hash "4ddb5679e6d3b467de9f71e4566001753f5876d78744d3ac96f7d0aa9e55e44d" :edb/db-before-t 2090 :edb/db-after-t 2091 :edb/tx-data [[13194139535403 50 #inst "2026-10-08T23:28:02.368Z" 13194139535403 true] [17592186192331 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/72329ee334988361174013e7df57452851cd826acf2ffc8b4cead6d750683e73.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2?$" 13194139535403 true] [17592186192331 1100 "Which of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^2$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/cdd0cbcb7e4fcb26862a5ab33958edac.png)" 13194139535403 false] [17592186192331 1145 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the whole plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n- Diagram III is a parabola that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535403 false] [17592186192331 1145 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents the whole plane $\\mathbb{R}^2$ without the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram II is a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\n- Diagram III is a parabola that does not pass through the origin. But every subspace must contain the origin. Therefore, it is *not* a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^2.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" 13194139535403 true] [17592186192332 1100 "Consider the subsets $\\; A=\\{ \\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3 : x^2=y \\}\\;$ and $\\; B= \\{ \\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle : t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" 13194139535403 true] [17592186192332 1100 "Consider the subsets $\\; A=\\{\\langle x, y, z \\rangle \\in \\mathbb{R}^3: x^2=y \\}\\;$ and $\\; B= \\{\\langle 3-t, -3+t, -6+2t \\rangle: t \\in \\mathbb{R} \\}.\\;$ Which of them is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$?" 13194139535403 false] [17592186192332 1145 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/b0bc18bcfd9d61510bed4ec4126688b599f5d3e6dbc883394ef7d5acbcf935f6.png) belongs to $A,$ but the vector ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/3f87fe8f3e9e256453a5d60981170b1655286081f9987bbeb7efc7624f6c6749.png) does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/75f1370c0eaafdcab1aa8e4cc673dbe6f71c61a0ad9f69a437ff9d8f4b115c42.png)\nwhich gives us\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}3-t=0 \\\\ -3+t=0 \\\\ -6+2t=0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3,$ then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B.$ Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139535403 true] [17592186192332 1145 "Let's analyze each of the subsets.\n\n- The set $A$ is not a subspace because it is not closed under scalar multiplication. For example, the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ belongs to $A$, but the vector $\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ does not lie in $A$ (because it does not satisfy $x^2=y$).\n- The set $B$ represents a line, and a line is a subspace if and only if it passes through the origin. If the origin belongs to this subset, we would have\n$\\begin{bmatrix}3 - t \\\\ -3 + t \\\\ -6 + 2t\\end{bmatrix}$\nwhich gives us\n${3 - t = 0; - 3 + t = 0; - 6 + 2t = 0$.\nWe see that $t=3$ is the solution to the three equations. So, if we choose $t=3$, then we find that $\\mathbf{0} \\in B$. Therefore, $B$ is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n\nIn conclusion, the correct answer is \"$B$ only\"." 13194139535403 false] [17592186192333 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/c66cdc8bbddfb00ce69f843b1ce1d12116b2dd82312a34abafa0fb13907110cf.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^3?$" 13194139535403 true] [17592186192333 1100 "Which of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $\\mathbb{R}^3$?\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/31239f102a0bab45ca4ad0b3cb2e9ccb.png)" 13194139535403 false] [17592186192333 1145 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I  represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram II  represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of  $\\mathbb{R}^3.$\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin ![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/a80f685053d7ac3f868728be13b5651c15bfb6c8f8f4394df75256b750a67485.png)  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535403 true] [17592186192333 1145 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I represents a plane that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n- Diagram II represents a line that passes through the origin. Therefore, it is also a subspace of $\\mathbb{R}^3$.\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace. The origin $\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}$ clearly lies outside the subset.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" 13194139535403 false] [17592186192605 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347467 13194139535403 true] [17592186192605 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347488 13194139535403 true] [17592186192605 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347509 13194139535403 true] [17592186192605 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347530 13194139535403 true] [17592186192606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347474 13194139535403 true] [17592186192606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347481 13194139535403 true] [17592186192606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347523 13194139535403 true] [17592186192606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347537 13194139535403 true] [17592186192606 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347544 13194139535403 true] [17592186192607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347453 13194139535403 true] [17592186192607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347460 13194139535403 true] [17592186192607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347495 13194139535403 true] [17592186192607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347502 13194139535403 true] [17592186192607 1020 #edb/ref 17592186347516 13194139535403 true] [17592186193139 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045463 13194139535403 true] [17592186193139 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/ebeebae27d24ae4dd342b9cf65f93fe58e77aa8dcd99a7cde0880f68378862fa.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}?$" 13194139535403 true] [17592186193139 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/MA/DATA/Lessons/4077/Source/Images/q-34318.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of $R^{2}$?" 13194139535403 false] [17592186193139 1145 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The vector $a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]$ lies inside given subset. But\n\n$$\n(-1)\\cdot a=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nlies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III does not represent a subspace since in does not contain the zero-vector.\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"None\"." 13194139535403 true] [17592186347453 1017 #edb/ref 17592186347454 13194139535403 true] [17592186347453 1089 "q-110449" 13194139535403 true] [17592186347453 1091 #edb/ref 17592186045465 13194139535403 true] [17592186347453 1100 "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/linear/14045249/assets/8c695ea0cb09807c22fa655182945e838a7c5e454680176add27748945d143e5.png)\n\nWhich of the diagrams shown above represent subspaces of ${R}^{2}$ or ${R}^{3}?$" 13194139535403 true] [17592186347453 1113 #uuid "def845b9-0c5b-5d87-865d-4200537b5ccb" 13194139535403 true] [17592186347453 1145 "Let's analyze each diagram in turn.\n\n- Diagram I does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram II does not represent a subspace. The origin $0=[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 0\\end{aligned}]$  clearly lies outside the subset.\n\n- Diagram III represents a line passing through the origin. Therefore, it is a subspace of  ${R}^{3}.$\n\nSo, the correct answer is \"III only.\"" 13194139535403 true] [17592186347454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347455 13194139535403 true] [17592186347454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347456 13194139535403 true] [17592186347454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347457 13194139535403 true] [17592186347454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347458 13194139535403 true] [17592186347454 1000 #edb/ref 17592186347459 13194139535403 true] [17592186347454 1040 #edb/ref 17592186045450 13194139535403 true] [17592186347454 1041 "selection" 13194139535403 true] [1759